WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.508
Day 27 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:09.055
Performance (3): AI generating and analyzing load tests.

00:00:09.605 --> 00:00:13.952
How to use an LLM to write a load script from a specification and to propose

00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:17.328
hypotheses about the results, with a method to verify each.

00:00:18.628 --> 00:00:22.919
Writing a load script from scratch and later reading a summary with hundreds of

00:00:22.919 --> 00:00:27.048
metrics are the two slowest parts of performance testing, and both are text.

00:00:27.348 --> 00:00:29.312
A model can speed both up.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:34.033
What it can't do is know what your traffic looks like, what your targets are or what

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.453
was happening on your servers during the test.

00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:42.259
If you don't give it that information, it fills it in with reasonable values and

00:00:42.259 --> 00:00:43.723
presents them confidently.

00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:47.823
The resulting script runs, and tests a load that isn't yours.

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:52.537
The resulting analysis sounds expert, and describes generic bottlenecks.

00:00:52.837 --> 00:00:57.398
This guide shows which inputs make AI useful in performance, how to review what it

00:00:57.398 --> 00:01:01.904
generates before spending a run and how to turn its analysis into hypotheses that

00:01:01.904 --> 00:01:02.627
get verified.

00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:05.625
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:06.175 --> 00:01:09.846
k6 and a practice API with an OpenAPI spec.

00:01:10.396 --> 00:01:15.035
An aggregated traffic profile (percentage per endpoint), never raw logs with

00:01:15.035 --> 00:01:15.890
personal data.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.975
Written service targets.

00:01:19.275 --> 00:01:20.555
This is the setup.

00:01:20.855 --> 00:01:25.072
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:26.372 --> 00:01:28.639
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:32.205
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:33.505 --> 00:01:34.541
Step 1.

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:36.597
Three required inputs.

00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:42.250
Give the model three inputs: the API spec, the aggregated traffic mix and the

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:42.780
targets.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.045
Without them it invents all three.

00:01:45.595 --> 00:01:48.581
The model needs three things to write a useful script.

00:01:48.881 --> 00:01:53.354
The API spec, to know the real endpoints, parameters and formats.

00:01:53.654 --> 00:01:58.802
The aggregated traffic mix, meaning what percentage of calls goes to each operation,

00:01:58.802 --> 00:02:01.191
taken from your logs with no user data.

00:02:01.491 --> 00:02:03.828
And the latency and error targets.

00:02:04.128 --> 00:02:06.928
If any of the three is missing, it will invent it.

00:02:07.228 --> 00:02:12.152
And it will do so plausibly, which is worse than doing it badly: an invented traffic

00:02:12.152 --> 00:02:14.673
mix doesn't look different from a real one.

00:02:15.973 --> 00:02:17.171
Step 2.

00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:19.935
Explicit requirements in the request.

00:02:20.485 --> 00:02:25.765
Request the script with explicit requirements: scenarios, think time, data from a

00:02:25.765 --> 00:02:29.219
file, environment variables, thresholds and timeouts.

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:34.919
Ask for the script with a list of requirements, not a general description: scenarios

00:02:34.919 --> 00:02:40.068
split by operation type, think time between actions of the same user, data read from

00:02:40.068 --> 00:02:45.034
a file, URLs and keys through environment variables, thresholds with your targets

00:02:45.034 --> 00:02:47.302
and timeouts suited to answer length.

00:02:47.602 --> 00:02:51.692
Each requirement corresponds to a frequent defect in generated scripts.

00:02:51.992 --> 00:02:56.963
Save the request as a template in the repo, just like the prompts from earlier days.

00:02:58.263 --> 00:02:59.346
Step 3.

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:01.274
Review with a checklist.

00:03:01.824 --> 00:03:07.607
Review it with a checklist before running: does the mix match? is there think time?

00:03:07.607 --> 00:03:12.483
is data non-repeating? zero secrets in code? are the thresholds yours?

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:16.705
Before running anything, review the script with five questions.

00:03:17.005 --> 00:03:19.678
Does the scenario mix match the one you passed?

00:03:19.978 --> 00:03:24.915
Is there think time between actions, or does each virtual user fire without pause?

00:03:25.215 --> 00:03:28.445
Does data vary, or is the same record repeated?

00:03:28.745 --> 00:03:31.000
Is any secret written in the code?

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.124
Are the thresholds yours, or did others appear?

00:03:34.424 --> 00:03:36.319
That's five minutes of reading.

00:03:36.619 --> 00:03:41.248
Running a wrong script costs an hour of testing, API budget and, above all,

00:03:41.248 --> 00:03:44.458
conclusions drawn from data that represents nothing.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:46.771
Step 4.

00:03:47.071 --> 00:03:48.873
Dry run and real run.

00:03:49.423 --> 00:03:53.962
Dry run with one user and one iteration to confirm it works, then the real run

00:03:53.962 --> 00:03:55.242
exporting the summary.

00:03:55.792 --> 00:03:59.080
Run first with one user and one iteration.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:04.693
It confirms the script works: that it authenticates, that responses are the expected

00:04:04.693 --> 00:04:06.337
ones and that checks pass.

00:04:06.637 --> 00:04:11.243
Finding an authentication error with a thousand virtual users is an expensive way to

00:04:11.243 --> 00:04:11.737
find out.

00:04:12.037 --> 00:04:15.999
Only then launch the real run and export the summary to a file.

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:21.389
That file, together with the exact time window of the test, is the input for the

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:22.025
next step.

00:04:23.325 --> 00:04:24.454
Step 5.

00:04:24.754 --> 00:04:26.904
Analysis with server metrics.

00:04:27.454 --> 00:04:31.927
For analysis, pass the summary along with server metrics from the same window.

00:04:32.227 --> 00:04:36.792
Ask for ranked hypotheses, with the supporting metric and how to confirm each.

00:04:37.342 --> 00:04:42.683
For analysis, give the model the test summary together with server metrics from the

00:04:42.683 --> 00:04:47.446
same window: CPU, memory, database connections, queue length, GPU usage if

00:04:47.446 --> 00:04:48.154
applicable.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:53.133
Ask for hypotheses ranked by likelihood, each with the supporting metric and the way

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:53.913
to confirm it.

00:04:54.213 --> 00:04:58.383
That format forces it to cite data instead of listing typical causes.

00:04:58.683 --> 00:05:03.022
A hypothesis that doesn't name a metric from your data gets discarded without

00:05:03.022 --> 00:05:03.980
further analysis.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.467
Step 6.

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:08.801
Verify the top hypothesis.

00:05:09.351 --> 00:05:12.570
Verify the top hypothesis with a targeted measurement.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.427
An unconfirmed bottleneck is a suspicion.

00:05:15.977 --> 00:05:20.416
Take the top-ranked hypothesis and design a targeted measurement to confirm or

00:05:20.416 --> 00:05:21.041
discard it.

00:05:21.341 --> 00:05:25.889
If it says the bottleneck is the connection pool, repeat the test with a larger pool

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:27.567
and see whether the knee moves.

00:05:27.867 --> 00:05:32.061
If it doesn't move, the hypothesis was wrong and you move to the next.

00:05:32.361 --> 00:05:36.160
Until a measurement confirms it, a bottleneck is a suspicion.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:41.063
Reporting it as a finding without verifying gets the team optimizing something that

00:05:41.063 --> 00:05:42.117
wasn't the problem.

00:05:43.417 --> 00:05:44.522
An example.

00:05:45.072 --> 00:05:50.211
A team asks a model for a script for its API with the spec, the traffic mix and the

00:05:50.211 --> 00:05:50.706
targets.

00:05:51.006 --> 00:05:55.568
The checklist review finds two problems: there's no think time between actions and

00:05:55.568 --> 00:05:59.851
the latency threshold is five hundred milliseconds, when their target was two

00:05:59.851 --> 00:06:00.296
seconds.

00:06:00.596 --> 00:06:01.806
They fix both.

00:06:02.106 --> 00:06:03.711
The dry run passes.

00:06:04.011 --> 00:06:07.404
The real run shows a knee at one hundred and twenty users.

00:06:07.704 --> 00:06:10.888
For analysis they hand over the summary and the server metrics.

00:06:11.188 --> 00:06:16.113
The top hypothesis: the database connection pool is exhausted, because active

00:06:16.113 --> 00:06:19.631
connections hit the maximum exactly when latency rises.

00:06:19.931 --> 00:06:24.194
They enlarge the pool, repeat, and the knee moves to one hundred and eighty.

00:06:24.494 --> 00:06:27.841
Hypothesis confirmed, with illustrative figures.

00:06:29.141 --> 00:06:30.803
The most common mistakes.

00:06:31.354 --> 00:06:35.187
Asking for the script without spec, traffic mix or targets.

00:06:35.737 --> 00:06:39.513
Running the generated script without reviewing it against the checklist.

00:06:40.063 --> 00:06:43.119
Asking for analysis with only the load tool's summary.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.969
Reporting an unverified hypothesis as the bottleneck.

00:06:48.269 --> 00:06:50.513
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:51.063 --> 00:06:54.479
The request includes spec, traffic mix and targets.

00:06:55.029 --> 00:06:58.120
The script passed the five-question checklist.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.017
There was a dry run before the real run.

00:07:01.567 --> 00:07:05.204
The top hypothesis was confirmed or discarded with a measurement.

00:07:06.504 --> 00:07:08.399
And where AI fell short.

00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:13.387
Without server metrics, it confidently diagnoses generic causes.

00:07:13.687 --> 00:07:16.766
The analysis is worth what the data you feed it is worth.

00:07:18.066 --> 00:07:19.810
That was day 27.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:25.084
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:25.084 --> 00:07:26.092
asked questions.

00:07:26.642 --> 00:07:31.788
